Local modifications to `epicare/envs.py`:
- Gymnasium API: imports `gymnasium` instead of `gym`; always builds Gymnasium
  spaces (`use_gymnasium` is accepted and ignored); `reset()` returns
  `(observation, {})` and `step()` returns
  `(observation, reward, terminated, truncated, info)`, and `reset()` calls
  `super().reset(seed=seed)`. Reaching `max_visits` (checked with `>=`
  instead of `==`) sets `truncated = True` and `terminated = False`
  (upstream set `terminated = True`); an adverse event or remission still
  sets `terminated = True`. The `use_gymnasium` default changed from `False`
  to `True`, which has no effect because the argument is ignored.
- Random numbers: episode randomness (patient modifiers, initial disease,
  remission, disease transitions, symptoms) comes from a per-instance
  `numpy.random.Generator` seeded by the constructor seed and re-seeded by
  `reset(seed=...)`. Upstream used the global NumPy random state, so a seed
  did not reproduce episodes. The constructor seed is also stored as
  `environment_seed`.
- `_adverse_event_probability` evaluates the multivariate-normal CDF with a
  fixed quasi-Monte Carlo seed (`seed=0`). Upstream used the global random
  state, so the expected rewards used by the SMART, Standard-of-Care, and
  Oracle reference policies varied slightly between processes. Sampled adverse
  events do not use this function.
- `reset()` returns the initial symptoms rounded to one decimal, as `step()`
  already did upstream; upstream returned them unrounded.
- Dtypes: `step()` casts the action to `int`, the reward to `float`, and
  observations to `float32`.
- On remission, `info["disease_pre_treatment"]` is the disease before the
  treatment; upstream reported `"Remission"`. On the other paths it is still
  the disease after the transition, as upstream.
- `time_to_remission` starts as `None` instead of `-1`. `handle_remission`
  records the time only when the attribute is `None`, so upstream never
  recorded it.
- The module-level `register(id="EpiCare-v0", ...)` call was removed.

Local modification to `epicare/policies.py`:
- The Q-values in the stochastic reference policy are converted to a float64
  array before arithmetic, which fixes their dtype; the values are unchanged.

Both modified files carry a header comment pointing to this NOTICE, as the
Apache License 2.0, section 4(b), requires.
